      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Amino acids and reducing sugars are the fundamental building blocks, as their amine and carbonyl groups react initially to form Schiff bases and Amadori products. The rate of this non-enzymatic browning is highly dependent on pH because the protonation state of the amino groups and carbonyls dictates their nucleophilicity. For instance, the reaction rate typically increases over a specific pH range, as both highly acidic and highly alkaline conditions can inhibit the necessary intermediate steps. Optimizing the pH is crucial for controlling the kinetics and extent of Maillard reactions in food systems.",
      "source": "target",
