    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, but I'm trying to get more specific for my analysis. Could you expand on the mechanism of the Amadori rearrangement itself? Also, regarding the pH part, how exactly does an alkaline environment accelerate the process compared to a slightly acidic one in terms of the molecular interaction?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Amadori rearrangement involves the isomerization of the initial unstable Schiff base adduct into a more stable ketoamine structure. This rearrangement requires proton transfer steps, where the intermediate undergoes tautomerization to shift the double bond and rearrange the carbonyl group. In an alkaline environment, hydroxide ions ($\\text{OH}^-$) facilitate the deprotonation of key acidic protons on the intermediate, increasing its reactivity and accelerating the necessary tautomeric shifts compared to conditions where proton availability is limited in slightly acidic solutions.",
      "source": "target",
